# Movement

Three separate systems. Never drive the same path or facing with more than one.

| Job | System | Stub |
|---|---|---|
| OOC A-to-B, waypoint replay, grind routes | `common/utility/simple_movement` | `.api/common/utility/simple_movement.lua` |
| Combat facing lock and brief pause for a cast | `common/utility/movement_handler` | `.api/common/utility/movement_handler.lua` |
| Navmesh pathfinding around geometry | `_G.NavLib` or Sentinel | `.api/third_party/sentinel_nav.lua` |

## simple_movement — waypoints

```lua
---@type simple_movement
local movement = require("common/utility/simple_movement")

movement:navigate(waypoints, false, true)   -- (waypoints[], is_loop?, start_from_beginning?)

core.register_on_update_callback(function()
    local reached = movement:process()      -- MUST run every frame
end)
```

- `:process()` every frame or the unit never advances. This is the most common bug.
- `:move_to_position(vec3)` for a single destination, `:stop()` / `:clear_navigation()` / `:pause()` / `:resume()` for control.
- Progress and state: `:is_moving()`, `:get_progress()` (0–100), `:get_current_index()`, `:get_remaining_waypoints()`, `:get_state()`.
- Tuning setters have documented ranges (threshold 1–10, final threshold 0.5–5, turn speed 0.05–0.5, look distance 5–50, smoothing subdivisions 1–20). Stay inside them.
- Prefer the look_at steering (`:set_use_look_at`) over the legacy turn constants.
- Flying is marked WIP in the stub; `flying_enabled` reads false. Do not build on it.

This is waypoint replay, **not** navmesh. It walks straight lines between points and will hug geometry.

## movement_handler — combat facing

```lua
---@type movement_handler
local movement_handler = require("common/utility/movement_handler")

core.register_on_render_callback(function()
    movement_handler:on_render()            -- MUST run every frame
end)

movement_handler:look_at_target(lock_duration, delay, target)
movement_handler:pause_movement(pause_duration, delay)
movement_handler:resume_movement(action_delay)
```

`:on_render()` every frame drives the timers. Without it, locks never release. Also available: `:look_at_position(lock_duration?, delay?, position)`, `:pause_movement_light(...)`, `:unlock_look_at(action_delay?)`, `:get_state()`.

This module is not a navigator. It does not travel anywhere.

## Navmesh

Probe for the global before using it — do not assume which is installed:

```lua
local nav = _G.NavLib
if not nav then return end
```

`_G.SentinelNavClient` is documented in `.api/third_party/sentinel_nav.lua`. Verify method names there before calling. Do not write a wrapper that "normalizes" the two APIs.

## Never invent

- A pathfinding method on `simple_movement`. It has none.
- A travel method on `movement_handler`.
- NavLib or Sentinel method names from generic pathfinding vocabulary.
- Mixing: no `simple_movement:navigate` while a navmesh client owns the same route, and no second facing controller alongside `movement_handler`.
